<title>CANADA: CAW sees new offer from Bombardier unit.</title>
<headline>CAW sees new offer from Bombardier unit.</headline>
<dateline>TORONTO 1997-06-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Canadian Auto Workers said on Friday that it expected to receive a new contract offer from Bombardier Inc's de Havilland unit shortly after midday today.</p>
<p>&quot;The second offer will send a signal as to whether this set of talks is heading closer to a strike rather than a settlement,&quot; said CAW president Buzz Hargrove.</p>
<p>Hargrove said de Havilland's first contract offer contained several items that could provoke a strike at the plane manufacturer's plant in Downsview, Ontario.</p>
<p>The company was demanding a five-year agreement, he said.</p>
<p>CAW locals 112 and 673 represent 3,800 workers at Downsview. The CAW set a strike deadline for 1000 EDT on June 23 if the two sides failed to reach an agreement.</p>
<p>((Robert Melnbardis, Reuters Toronto bureau 416-941-8100))</p>
